mistakes.text:
using ln(1 + h) approx h for large h (ln 2 = 0.693,
   not 1); confusing ln with log base 10 in software (np.log is the
   natural log, np.log10 is base 10).

d/dx e^x = x e^(x-1) (WRONG: power rule misuse);
   d/dx 2^x = x 2^(x-1) (WRONG; correct 2^x ln 2);
   d/dx ln(x^2) = 1/x^2 (WRONG; correct 2/x). Degrees instead of
   radians in trig derivatives.

"the terms get closer to each other, so it converges" is true
   for sequences (Cauchy) but the analogous idea for series is wrong:
   terms -> 0 does not make a series converge.

using the series outside its interval of convergence
   (e.g. ln(1+x) series at x = 3); forgetting n! in the denominator;
   forgetting (x - a) when a != 0.

arithmetic slips (re-check by substituting); multiplying a
   row by 0; operating on only the coefficient side and not the RHS;
   dividing by a (near-)zero pivot without swapping rows.

ordering eigenvectors and eigenvalues inconsistently;
   assuming every matrix is diagonalizable; using the formula for
   non-square matrices (use SVD instead).

dividing by |u| instead of |u|^2 (or forgetting to square);
   using it with non-orthogonal basis vectors one by one for a
   multi-dimensional subspace (see below).

forgetting the square root; dropping a squared term;
   adding instead of subtracting coordinates in the midpoint formula
   (it is an average).

using a point on the plane in place of a normal vector;
   the plane equation's "d" sign conventions (a x + b y + c z + d' = 0
   vs = d): use a consistent form.

forgetting to treat the other variables as constants;
   thinking dy/dx (a total derivative) and f_x are the same; losing the
   product rule when both variables appear.

forgetting to compute D at the specific point; using
   f_xx f_yy instead of f_xx f_yy - f_xy^2; claiming a max/min when D = 0;
   ignoring the boundary for global extremes.

wrong limits after swapping the order (draw the region!);
   constant limits where a variable limit is needed; forgetting the
   integrand's Jacobian in a change of coordinates.

forgetting the constant; losing equilibrium solutions;
   dropping absolute values inside logs without justification;
   attempting to separate an equation that does not factorize
   (e.g. dy/dx = x + y is NOT separable; it is linear).

using list slicing in recursion without realizing it creates O(n)
   copies (fine here but affects constants); using < instead of <= in the merge,
   which breaks stability.

reading it as "we can never tell whether a program
   halts" (we can for many); confusing undecidable with "takes very long";
   thinking more powerful hardware or more memory would solve it.

leaving some (state, symbol) pairs undefined in a DFA;
   forgetting that the empty string is accepted iff q0 is in F; not
   making the machine remember enough (state meaning must be clear).

choosing a w that depends on a particular split;
   forgetting condition |xy| <= p; trying to use it to show a language IS
   regular; choosing w too short (< p).

averaging percentages or ratios without weights (Module 26);
   reporting the mean of heavily skewed data (income) as "typical";
   averaging ordinal codes (1-5 satisfaction) without noting the
   assumption.

using sigma vs sigma^2 inconsistently (software scale =
   SD); applying the empirical rule to non-normal data; treating the
   density value as a probability; adding SDs instead of variances;
   forgetting to standardize before using tables; assuming "large
   sample" makes the DATA normal (only the MEAN becomes approximately
   normal).

E[1/X] = 1/E[X] (WRONG); E[X^2] = (E[X])^2 (WRONG; the gap is the
   variance); E[XY] = E[X] E[Y] without independence (WRONG);
   computing E of a continuous variable by summing.

Var(a X) = a Var(X) (WRONG: a^2); adding standard deviations;
   Var(X - Y) = Var X - Var Y (WRONG: variances ADD for independent
   differences); applying Var(sum) = sum Var to dependent variables;
   using population and sample formulas interchangeably (Module 31).

see the list 'What a p-value is not' in this
   section.
COMMON MISTAKES: the dichotomy p = 0.049 "significant" vs p = 0.051 "not";
   reporting only "p < 0.05" without estimate and interval; p-hacking
   (trying many analyses and reporting the one with p < 0.05);
   one-sided p after peeking.

expecting variance to fall like 1/B for correlated trees;
   thinking bagging reduces bias (it keeps bias about the same;
   averaging does not fix systematic error).

univariate feature screening (correlation with the target) would
   discard both XOR features although together they determine y perfectly;
   assuming more data makes a linear model fit a nonlinear pattern.

conditions.text:
- a^0 = 1 requires a != 0 (0^0 is an indeterminate form in limits;
     in many programming/combinatorial contexts it is DEFINED as 1;
     Python: 0**0 = 1).
   - For even n, the n-th root of a negative number is not real.
   - (a^m)^n = a^(mn) is NOT a^(m^n).
   - (a + b)^n != a^n + b^n.

f continuous on [a, b] (or at least bounded with finitely
   many jumps) guarantees the integral exists. It is a NUMBER (unlike the
   indefinite integral, a family of functions).
   Properties:
     int[a..a] f = 0;  int[b..a] f = - int[a..b] f;
     int[a..b] (c f + g) = c int f + int g;
     int[a..b] f = int[a..c] f + int[c..b] f;
     if f >= g on [a,b] then int f >= int g;
     |int[a..b] f| <= int[a..b] |f|.

only for square matrices. Properties:
     det(AB) = det(A) det(B);  det(A^T) = det(A);
     det(A^(-1)) = 1/det(A);   det(cA) = c^n det(A)  (n x n matrix; NOT
     c det A);
     swapping two rows flips the sign; multiplying a row by c multiplies
     det by c; adding a multiple of one row to another changes nothing;
     a zero row, or two equal/proportional rows => det = 0;
     det(A + B) != det(A) + det(B).
     A is invertible <=> det(A) != 0.
     det(A) = product of eigenvalues (Module 9).

holds for any matrix (any field). Also: rank(A) <=
   min(m, n); rank(A) = rank(A^T); rank(AB) <= min(rank A, rank B);
   rank(A^T A) = rank(A).

assumes linear structure; sensitive to scaling and
   outliers; variance is not the same as "importance for prediction";
   components are uncorrelated but not necessarily independent.

these are the standard (centered, axis-aligned) forms;
   general quadrics need rotation and translation (eigen-decomposition
   of the quadratic form, F9.4).

the unknown is a function of ONE independent variable;
   the order is the highest derivative present; existence and uniqueness
   need conditions (F15.2).

needs PAIRED data of equal length. Units are (unit of x) x
   (unit of y), so the magnitude is hard to interpret. Properties:
   Cov(aX + b, cY + d) = a c Cov(X, Y);
   Cov(X, Y) = Cov(Y, X); Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y + 2 Cov(X, Y);
   independent => Cov = 0 (converse FALSE).

WHY THE LOGARITHM
   (1) ln is increasing, so L and l have the SAME maximizer;
   (2) it turns the product of n factors into a sum: far easier to
       differentiate (F3.8) and add;
   (3) numerical stability: a product of 1,000 small densities underflows
       to 0, a sum of logs does not;
   (4) the negative log-likelihood is the usual LOSS function (cross-entropy
       for classification, squared error for Gaussian regression).
   Independence is what makes L a product; for dependent data use the
   joint density (chain rule).

identifiability can be global or local;
   near-non-identifiability (weak identification) is also harmful in
   practice.

K must be a valid (positive semi-definite) kernel;
   features should be scaled; cost grows with the number of training
   points.
